# 3-2-3- Reliability test of variables 

One of the most significant topics in the analysis of time series data is the discussion of stationarity. Prior to discussing mana, it is necessary to distinguish between strict mana and weak mana. Generally, a strictly Mana process is one in which the probability of the sequence $\{Y t\}$ is equal to the probability of the sequence $\{Y t+k\}$ for every $k$. Weak mean processes are defined as follows: a time series that meets the following three conditions is said to be a weak mean process. The following conditions must be met: the mean, variance, and autocorrelation coefficients must remain constant over time. As a result, this means:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& E\left(Y_{t}\right)=\mu \\
& \operatorname{Var}\left(Y_{t}\right)=E\left(Y_{t}-\mu\right)^{2}=\sigma^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\operatorname{Corr}\left(Y_{t}, Y_{t-k}\right)=\frac{\gamma_{k}}{\sigma^{2}}=\rho_{k}
$$

To estimate the coefficients of the desired models, traditional and usual econometric methods were used. As a result, variables in the desired model are assumed to be weak, or stable.

# 7. CONCLUSION WITH SUMMARY 

We conclude by summarizing our findings in Tables 2 to 4. Each table considers one of the three separation properties. The right column describes the property of the completion of a distributive lattice, the left column the corresponding property satisfied by the lattice, and the center column the dual condition on the Priestley space. Within each table, the conditions are arranged from the weakest to the strongest.

For $\mathrm{A} \in$ DLat, recall the following notions from the end of Section 4 relating to subfitness:

- A is $\mathcal{B} \mathcal{L}$-subfit if $\mathcal{B} \mathcal{L} A$ is subfit (equivalently, $p \mathcal{O}(A$ is subfit);
- A is $\mathcal{J}$-subfit ${ }^{10}$ if $\mathcal{M}$ is subfit;

For $\mathrm{A} \in$ DLat, consider the following definitions relating to regularity:

- A is $\mathcal{D} M$-regular if $\mathcal{D} M A$ is regular;
- A is $\mathcal{B} \mathcal{L}$-regular if $\mathcal{B} \mathcal{L} A$ is regular (equivalently, $p \mathcal{O}(A$ is regular);

[^0]
[^0]:    ${ }^{10}$ This should not be confused with the notion of ideally subfit introduced in [DIM21, Def. 2.13] (which is of novel interest only when $A$ is unbounded).

![table_1](table_1)

Table 2. Summary for subfitness

For Dedekind-MacNeille completions that are distributive, the notion of $\mathcal{D} \mathcal{M}$-regular is simply $\mathcal{B} \mathcal{L}$-regular plus subfit (Proposition 5.7). Also, the notions of $\mathcal{J}$ - and $(\cdot)^{\sigma}$-regular coincide with being Boolean (Propositions 5.21 and 5.22).

