# 3.1 Multilevel Binary LR for Binary Responses 

The multilevel binary LR [31] was initially designed to include random effects associated with items, accounting for variations among items and offering more accurate DIF estimates compared to the traditional LR [18]. We have modified its notation to allow the incorporation of more than one focal group and of random effects from higherlevel clusters in hierarchical data (e.g., access to healthcare, geographical locations, age groups, education levels).

In this modified version, the level-1 submodel pertains to responses to a studied item from individual respondents, indexed by $p$, within a level-2 stratum, indexed by $i$. This two-level binary LR model is specified as follows:

Level 1:

$$
\operatorname{logit}\left(\pi_{p i}\right)=\log \left(\frac{\pi_{p i}}{1-\pi_{p i}}\right)=\beta_{0 i}+\beta_{1 i} \theta_{p}+\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\mathbf{2 i}} \boldsymbol{g}_{\boldsymbol{p}}
$$

Level 2:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \beta_{0 i}=\delta_{00}+U_{0 i} \\
& \beta_{1 i}=\delta_{10}+U_{1 i} \\
& \boldsymbol{\beta}_{\mathbf{2 i}}=\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\mathbf{2 0}}+U_{2 i}
\end{aligned}
$$

where
$\pi_{p i}$ is the probability for respondent $p$ to provide the targeted response to the studied item (i.e., the "probability targeted");
$\beta_{0 i}$ denotes the log-odds of the "probability targeted" in the reference group;
$\beta_{1 i}$ is the effect of respondents' latent trait on the log-odds of the "probability targeted" in the reference group;
$\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\mathbf{2 i}}$ is a vector for the deviation effect in a focal group from the reference group;
$\theta_{p}$ is the latent trait of respondent $p$ (e.g., it could be proxied by a continuous variable concerning the respondent's overall health status);
$\boldsymbol{g}_{\boldsymbol{p}}$ is a vector containing dummy-coded variables representing the group membership of respondent $p$, specified as $\left[g_{p 1} g_{p 2} \cdots g_{p J}\right]\left(g_{p j}=1\right.$ for being in the target group, indexed by $j=1,2, \cdots, J$ );
$\delta_{00}$ is the average log-odds of the "probability targeted;"
$\delta_{10}$ is the average effect associated with respondents' latent trait;
$\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\mathbf{2 0}}$ is a vector for the average effect associated with being in each focal group;
$U_{0 i}$ is the random effect associated with level-2 clusters on the intercept $\beta_{0 i}$;
$U_{1 i}$ and $U_{2 i}$ denote the random effect associated with level-2 clusters on the slopes $\beta_{1 i}$ and $\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\mathbf{2 i}}$, respectively;

$U_{0 i}, U_{1 i}$, and $U_{2 i}$ follow a multivariate normal distribution with the mean being 0 .
The level-1 submodel of the multilevel binary LR is a binary LR model, similar to the one in traditional LR but without an ability-group interaction term. It is limited to binary response data analysis. The level-2 submodel in the multilevel binary LR accounts for random effects associated with higher-level clusters. When using this multilevel binary LR, the studied item exhibits DIF if any coefficient value in the vector $\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\mathbf{2 i}}$ is significantly different from 0 .

# C. 2 Discussions on 2SLS with $\ell_{2}$-Regularization 

In this section, we briefly discuss a generalization of our analysis to the case where the 2SLS estimator is regularized by the $\ell_{2}$ penalty (Ridge 2SLS). For this case, the bi-level optimization problem (6) is modified as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\min _{\boldsymbol{\beta}} & \mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\beta})=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(y_{i}-\boldsymbol{z}_{i}^{\top} \boldsymbol{\Theta} \boldsymbol{\beta}\right)^{2}+\frac{1}{2} \lambda\|\boldsymbol{\beta}\|^{2} \\
\text { where } & \hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}:=\underset{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}{\arg \min } \quad \frac{1}{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{j}-\boldsymbol{z}_{j}^{\top} \boldsymbol{\Theta}\right)^{2}+\frac{1}{2} \tau\|\boldsymbol{\Theta}\|_{F}^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

where $\lambda, \tau \geq 0$ are regularization parameters. To solve this problem, the GD updates in (7) is modified as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t+1)} & =\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t)}-\eta\left[\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{Z} \boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t)}-\boldsymbol{X}\right)+\tau \boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t)}\right] \\
\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(t+1)} & =\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(t)}-\alpha\left[\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t) \top} \boldsymbol{Z}^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{Z} \boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t)} \boldsymbol{\beta}^{(t)}-\boldsymbol{Y}\right)+\lambda \boldsymbol{\beta}^{(t)}\right]
\end{aligned}
$$

The only difference between (7) and (61) is the additional terms $\tau \boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(t)}$ in (61a), and $\lambda \boldsymbol{\beta}^{(t)}$ in (61b). The convergence analysis of the $\ell_{2}$-regularized GD updates in (61) can be conducted in a similar manner as in Theorem 3.1. The only difference is that the convergence rate $\Lambda$ is now determined by the spectral radiuses of $\boldsymbol{I}-\eta\left(\boldsymbol{Z}^{\top} \boldsymbol{Z}+\tau \boldsymbol{I}\right)$ and $\boldsymbol{I}-\alpha\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}^{\top} \boldsymbol{Z}^{\top} \boldsymbol{Z} \hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}+\lambda \boldsymbol{I}\right)$, respectively.

With the same configuration as in Theorem 3.2 but adding $p+1$ attention heads in the second layer (i.e. the second layer needs $3 p+3$ heads), we can show that transformers are able to implement the $\ell_{2}$-regularized GD updates in (61). The proof follows directly from Appendix B.2, with the construction of the new attention heads in the second layer as follows.
For $m=2 p+2+k, k=1, \ldots, p$, define $\boldsymbol{Q}_{m}^{(2)}, \boldsymbol{K}_{m}^{(2)}, \boldsymbol{V}_{m}^{(2)}$ such that:

$$
\boldsymbol{Q}_{m}^{(2)} \boldsymbol{h}_{i}^{(1)}=\left[\begin{array}{l}
1 \\
\mathbf{0}
\end{array}\right], \boldsymbol{K}_{m}^{(2)} \boldsymbol{h}_{i}^{(1)}=\left[\begin{array}{l}
1 \\
\mathbf{0}
\end{array}\right], \boldsymbol{V}_{m}^{(2)} \boldsymbol{h}_{i}^{(1)}=-\eta \tau \sum_{l=1}^{q} \Theta_{l k}^{(0)} \boldsymbol{e}_{D_{0}+(k-1) q+l}
$$

For $m=3 p+3$, define $\boldsymbol{Q}_{3 p+3}^{(2)}, \boldsymbol{K}_{3 p+3}^{(2)}, \boldsymbol{V}_{3 p+3}^{(2)}$ such that:

$$
\boldsymbol{Q}_{3 p+3}^{(2)} \boldsymbol{h}_{i}^{(1)}=\left[\begin{array}{l}
1 \\
\mathbf{0}
\end{array}\right], \boldsymbol{K}_{3 p+3}^{(2)} \boldsymbol{h}_{i}^{(1)}=\left[\begin{array}{l}
1 \\
\mathbf{0}
\end{array}\right], \boldsymbol{V}_{3 p+3}^{(2)} \boldsymbol{h}_{i}^{(1)}=-\alpha \lambda \sum_{l=1}^{q} \beta_{l}^{(0)} \boldsymbol{e}_{D_{0}+q p+l}
$$

Then the remaining proof follows exactly the same as Appendix B.2. This result indicates that transformers are potentially capable of handling multicollinearity in IV regression problem. We conduct experiments to validate this and the results are shown in Appendix C.3.

# 2.1 Stochastic orders 

The quantile function for a random variable $X$ with distribution function $F_{X}$ is defined as:

$$
\operatorname{VaR}_{p}(X):=F_{X}^{-1}(p)=\inf \left\{x \in \mathbb{R} \mid F_{X}(x) \geq p\right\}, \quad p \in(0,1)
$$

Now, we present several pertinent definitions of univariate stochastic orders that will be utilized in subsequent discussions.

Definition 2.1. Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with distribution functions $F_{X}$ and $F_{Y}$, density functions $f_{X}$ and $f_{Y}$, and survival functions $\bar{F}_{X}$ and $\bar{F}_{Y}$, respectively. Then $X$ is said to be smaller than $Y$ in the:
(i) usual stochastic order (denoted by $X \leq_{\mathrm{st}} Y$ ) if $\bar{F}_{X}(t) \leq \bar{F}_{Y}(t)$ for all $t \in \mathbb{R}$;
(ii) excess wealth order (denoted by $X \leq_{\mathrm{ew}} Y$ ) if $\mathbb{E}\left[\left(X-F_{X}^{-1}(p)\right)_{+}\right] \leq \mathbb{E}\left[\left(Y-F_{Y}^{-1}(p)\right)_{+}\right]$for all $0<p<1$, where $x_{+}=\max (0, x)$;
(iii) star order (denoted by $X \leq_{*} Y$ ) if $F_{Y}^{-1}(p) / F_{X}^{-1}(p)$ is increasing in $p \in(0,1)$, when the two random variables are non-negative;
(iv) expected proportional shortfall order (denoted by $X \leq_{\mathrm{ps}} Y$ ) if $\operatorname{EPS}_{p}(X) \leq \operatorname{EPS}_{p}(Y)$ for all $p \in$ $D_{X} \cap D_{Y}$, when the two random variables are non-negative, where $D_{X}=\left\{p \in(0,1): F_{X}^{-1}(p)>0\right\}$, $D_{Y}=\left\{p \in(0,1): F_{Y}^{-1}(p)>0\right\}$. Here,

$$
\operatorname{EPS}_{p}(X)=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\frac{X-\operatorname{VaR}_{p}(X)}{\operatorname{VaR}_{p}(X)}\right)_{+}\right] \quad \text { and } \operatorname{EPS}_{p}(Y)=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\frac{Y-\operatorname{VaR}_{p}(Y)}{\operatorname{VaR}_{p}(Y)}\right)_{+}\right]
$$

In Definition 2.1, (i)-(iii) are referenced in Shaked \& Shanthikumar (2007), and (iv) can be found in Belzunce et al. (2012). It is also known that both of the star order and the expected proportional shortfall order are scaled invariant and the former implies the latter. Interested readers can refer to the monographs Shaked \& Shanthikumar (2007) and Belzunce et al. (2015) for more detailed discussions.

Next, for the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}=\left(X_{1}, \ldots, X_{n}\right)$, we introduce some multivariate stochastic orders and dependence notions, indicating that in some stochastic sense, larger values of one random vector are associated with larger or smaller values of another random vector.

Definition 2.2. (Shaked \& Shanthikumar, 2007) Let $\boldsymbol{X}=\left(X_{1}, \ldots, X_{n}\right)$ and $\boldsymbol{Y}=\left(Y_{1}, \ldots, Y_{n}\right)$ be two random vectors with joint distribution functions $F$ and $G$, respectively. Then
(i) the random variable $\left\{X_{i}, i \in A^{c}\right\}$, is said to be right-tail increasing (decreasing) in $\left\{X_{j}, j \in A\right\}$, (denoted by $\left\{X_{i}, i \in A^{c}\right\} \uparrow_{\mathrm{RT}[\mathrm{RTD}]} \mid\left\{X_{j}, j \in A\right\}$ ) if $\mathbb{P}\left(X_{i}>x_{i}, i \in A^{c} \mid X_{j}>x_{j}, j \in A\right)$ increases (decreases) in $x_{j}$, where $A$ is a subset of $\{1, \ldots, n\}$ with at least one element, and $A^{c}$ denotes the complement of $A$;

(ii) the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}$ is said to be smaller than $\boldsymbol{Y}$ in the multivariate hazard rate order (denoted by $\boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{hr}} \boldsymbol{Y}$ ) if $\bar{F}(\boldsymbol{x}) \bar{G}(\boldsymbol{y}) \leq \bar{F}(\boldsymbol{x} \wedge \boldsymbol{y}) \bar{G}(\boldsymbol{x} \vee \boldsymbol{y})$ for all $\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$;
(iii) the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}$ is said to be smaller than $\boldsymbol{Y}$ in the weak multivariate hazard rate order (denoted by $\boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{whr}} \boldsymbol{Y}$ ) if $\bar{G}(\boldsymbol{x}) / \bar{F}(\boldsymbol{x})$ is increasing in $\boldsymbol{x} \in\{\boldsymbol{x}: \bar{G}(\boldsymbol{x})>0\}$;
(iv) the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}$ is said to be smaller than $\boldsymbol{Y}$ in the usual stochastic order (denoted by $\boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{st}} \boldsymbol{Y}$ ) if $\mathbb{E}[h(\boldsymbol{X})] \leq \mathbb{E}[h(\boldsymbol{Y})]$ for all bounded increasing $h: \mathbb{R}^{n} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$;
(v) the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}$ is said to be multivariate totally positive of order 2 (denoted by $\mathrm{MTP}_{2}$ ) if $f(\boldsymbol{x}) f(\boldsymbol{y}) \leq f(\boldsymbol{x} \wedge \boldsymbol{y}) f(\boldsymbol{x} \vee \boldsymbol{y})$ holds for all $\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$;
(vi) the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}$ is said to be smaller than $\boldsymbol{Y}$ in the multivariate likelihood ratio order (denoted by $\boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{lr}} \boldsymbol{Y}$ ) if $f(\boldsymbol{x}) g(\boldsymbol{y}) \leq f(\boldsymbol{x} \wedge \boldsymbol{y}) g(\boldsymbol{x} \vee \boldsymbol{y})$ for all $\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{y} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$;
(vii) the random vector $\hat{\boldsymbol{X}}_{i}=\left(X_{1}, \ldots, X_{i-1}, X_{i+1}, \ldots, X_{n}\right)$ is said to be stochastically increasing in $X_{i}$ (denoted by $\hat{\boldsymbol{X}}_{i} \uparrow_{\mathrm{SI}} X_{i}$ ) if the conditional distribution $\left\{\left(X_{1}, \ldots, X_{i-1}, X_{i+1}, \ldots, X_{n} \mid X_{i}=x_{i}\right)\right\}$ is stochatically increasing as $x_{i}$ increases;
(viii) the random vector $\boldsymbol{X}$ is said to be positive dependent through the stochastic order (or PDS) if $\hat{\boldsymbol{X}}_{i} \uparrow_{\mathrm{SI}} X_{i}$ for $i \in\{1, \ldots, n\}$.

According to Hu et al. (2003), the following relationships hold:

$$
\boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{lr}} \boldsymbol{Y} \Longrightarrow \boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{lr}} \boldsymbol{Y} \Longrightarrow \boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{whr}} \boldsymbol{Y} \text { and } \boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{lr}} \boldsymbol{Y} \Longrightarrow \boldsymbol{X} \leq_{\mathrm{st}} \boldsymbol{Y}
$$

# 2.2 Reference strategy 

A common practice in order execution brokerage is that clients may come forward to brokers with their own preferred strategies for benchmarking. These benchmark strategies can be either strategies suggested by elite investors or commonly used ones such as TWAP strategies. We shall refer to these pre-specified benchmark strategies as reference strategies. We consider the reference strategies that can be represented by a deterministic function $\left(R_{t}\right)_{t \in[0, T]}$ with $R_{0} \equiv x_{0}$ and $R_{T} \equiv A$ hereafter. Moreover, we assume that $R_{t}$ is a differentiable a.e. function. The broker's incentive of executing client's order is thus to maximize his own expected $\mathrm{P} \& \mathrm{~L}$ excess to that of the reference strategy, marked-to-market. Specifically, by disregarding the price impact and slippages incurred from order execution, the stock price $S_{t}^{0}$ reads

$$
S_{t}^{0}=S_{0}+\mu t+\sigma W_{t}
$$

The marked-to-market $\mathrm{P} \& \mathrm{~L} \Pi^{R}$ for the reference strategy $R$ is evaluated as

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Pi^{R} & :=\int_{0}^{T}\left(-S_{t}^{0}\right) \mathrm{d} R_{t} \\
& =-\left(S_{t}^{0} R_{t}\right) \int_{0}^{T}+\int_{0}^{T} R_{t}\left(\mu \mathrm{~d} t+\sigma \mathrm{d} W_{t}\right) \\
& =-A S_{T}^{0}+x_{0} S_{0}+\int_{0}^{T} \mu R_{t} \mathrm{~d} t+\int_{0}^{T} \sigma R_{t} \mathrm{~d} W_{t} \\
& =\left(x_{0}-A\right) S_{0}+\int_{0}^{T} \mu\left(R_{t}-A\right) \mathrm{d} t+\int_{0}^{T} \sigma\left(R_{t}-A\right) \mathrm{d} W_{t}
\end{aligned}
$$

Hence, the broker's excess $\mathrm{P} \& \mathrm{~L} \tilde{\Pi}$, defined by the difference between $\Pi$ and $\Pi^{R}$, is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{\Pi}:= & \Pi-\Pi^{R} \\
= & -\beta\left(x_{T}-A\right)^{2}+\int_{0}^{T}\left(\mu\left(x_{t}-R_{t}\right)+\rho \sigma m\left(v_{t}\right)-\eta v_{t}^{2}-\gamma v_{t}\left(x_{t}-A\right)\right) \mathrm{d} t \\
& +\int_{0}^{T} \sigma\left(x_{t}-R_{t}\right) \mathrm{d} W_{t}+\int_{0}^{T}\left(\eta v_{t}+\gamma\left(x_{t}-A\right)\right) m\left(v_{t}\right) \mathrm{d} Z_{t}
\end{aligned}
$$

The broker's goal is thus to maximize his expected excess $\mathrm{P} \& \mathrm{~L}$ in a risk aversion manner which we recast as a utility maximization problem in the section that follows.

## 1 Introduction

The promotion time cure model (PTCM) is one of the most important models in survival analysis, but it has not yet been studied much in the literature (Amico and Keilegom, 2018). The PTCM is constructed by motivating biological considerations, which assumes that after initial treatment the time to recurrence of cancer is the result of a latent process of the residual tumor cells (i.e. clonogenic cells) propagating into a newly detectable tumor (Yakovlev, 1996). As shown in Yakovlev (1996) and Chen et al. (1999), the construction of the PTCM dependents on a latent variable $N$ that is the number of clonogenic cells left active in a patient after initial treatment. Assume that $N$ is Poisson distributed with mean $\theta>0$, i.e. $\mathbb{P}(N=k)=\theta^{k} e^{-\theta} / k!, k \in \mathbb{N}$. Let another latent variable $Z_{j}(j=1, \ldots, N)$ be the random time for the $j$-th clonogenic cell to produce a detectable tumor mass. Given $N$, the variables $Z_{j}$ are independently and identically distributed with cumulative distribution function (cdf) $F(t)=1-S(t)$. Here $F(t)$ is the promotion time distribution of any clonogenic cell and $S(t)$ is its corresponding survival function. The time to tumor recurrence can be defined by the random variable $T=\min _{0 \leq j \leq N}\left\{Z_{j}\right\}$, i.e. tumor recurrence when the one of the clonogenic cells becomes activated, where $\mathbb{P}\left(Z_{0}=\infty\right)=1$ (i.e. no tumor recurrence in a finite time). Note that the time to tumor recurrence $T$ of a patient is observable, but $N$ and $Z_{j}$ are unobservable latent variables. The survival function of the population is the probability of no newly detectable tumor by time $t$ given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
S_{p o p}(t) & =\mathbb{P}(N=0)+\mathbb{P}\left(Z_{1}>t, \ldots ., Z_{N}>t, N>0\right) \\
& =e^{-\theta}+\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \mathbb{P}\left(Z_{1}>t, \ldots ., Z_{N=k}>t\right) \mathbb{P}(N=k) \\
& =e^{-\theta}+\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} S(t)^{k} \frac{\theta^{k} e^{-\theta}}{k!}=e^{-\theta(1-S(t))}=e^{-\theta F(t)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Covariates can be introduced in the parameter $\theta$ and may also be introduced in the proper baseline distribution function $F(t)$. However, it is not clear how to include clonogenic cell data information in the PTCM (1) for better progression-free survival prediction or potentially for the identification of subclonal driver genes predictive of survival. Motivated by this, we propose the following generalized promotion time cure model (GPTCM) to integrate multiscale data, i.e. cancer patient data on multiple biological scales: individual-level survival data, cellular-level cell type proportions data, and subcellular-level cell-type-specific genetic variables.

